🧠 Golden rule (memorize this)

      Never drive DUT signals after await ReadOnly()
      If you need to write again, wait for a new clock edge.

      This is exactly how UVM separates sampling vs driving.
      
      
      Always structure driver like this:

      RisingEdge → drive signals

      ReadOnly → sample signals

      RisingEdge → drive cleanup
        

    

🎯 Golden rule (APB + cocotb)

   PSLVERR and PRDATA must be sampled in the same cycle as PREADY

   Not one cycle later.
   
🔁 Write vs Sample vs Read (in cocotb / simulator terms)

These are NOT Python concepts.
They are simulation-time phases.

Think in clocked RTL timing, not functions.

1️⃣ WRITE = Drive signals (active region)

What it means

You are driving values onto DUT inputs.

When it happens

Before or at a clock edge

In the active phase of the simulator

Examples

dut.psel.value = 1
dut.paddr.value = addr
dut.penable.value = 0


UVM equivalent

drv_if.psel   <= 1;
drv_if.paddr  <= addr;


📌 Rule

Writes must NOT happen during ReadOnly()
You can only drive signals in active phases.

2️⃣ SAMPLE = Observe stable signals (read-only region)

What it means

You are observing DUT outputs when they are guaranteed stable.

When it happens

After all RTL has settled for that timestep

In the ReadOnly phase

Examples

await ReadOnly()
slverr = int(dut.pslverr.value)
data   = int(dut.prdata.value)


UVM equivalent

@(posedge clk);
#0;               // wait for NBA updates
slverr = vif.pslverr;


📌 Rule

Sampling must happen in ReadOnly()
Otherwise you may see old or transient values.

3️⃣ READ = Transaction type, not timing

This is where the confusion happens.

❌ “Read” does NOT mean “sample”

A READ transaction is an APB operation:

pwrite = 0

prdata is returned

await drv.read(addr)


This function contains all 3 phases:

Write (drive APB signals)

Sample (capture prdata, pslverr)

Complete (drop bus)

📌 READ ≠ SAMPLE

🧠 Timeline of ONE APB READ (exactly your case)
Cycle N:
  Driver WRITE:
    psel=1, pwrite=0, penable=0

Cycle N+1:
  Driver WRITE:
    penable=1

Cycle N+2:
  DUT:
    pready=1
    prdata valid
    pslverr valid

  Monitor SAMPLE (ReadOnly):
    sees prdata, pslverr

  Driver SAMPLE (ReadOnly):
    MUST sample here too 👈

Cycle N+3:
  Driver WRITE:
    psel=0, penable=0

❌ What went wrong in your failing runs

You did this:

await RisingEdge(clk)
if pready:
    break

await ReadOnly()
slverr = dut.pslverr


❌ That samples one cycle later
❌ pslverr already returned to 0
❌ Monitor sampled correctly, driver didn’t   


👉 In pure UVM philosophy:

Driver drives

Monitor samples

Scoreboard checks

👉 In cocotb (and many practical UVM drivers):

Driver also samples only to know when the transaction completed and whether it errored

Monitor is the authority for functional observation

❗ The driver samples handshake and status, not functional data

1️⃣ What the driver samples

The driver samples only control / completion info:

Signal	Why driver samples
PREADY	Know when transfer finished
PSLVERR	Know if transaction succeeded
PRDATA	(Optional) only if returning value to test

📌 The driver does NOT own correctness

It does not compare expected vs actual

It does not update memory model

It does not track coverage

That’s the monitor + scoreboard’s job.

2️⃣ Why the driver must sample PSLVERR

Your failing bug proves this perfectly.

What happened:

Monitor saw PSLVERR=1

Driver sampled one cycle later

PSLVERR already deasserted

Driver thought error didn’t happen

Assertion failed

📌 If the driver doesn’t sample PSLVERR at the exact completion cycle, it cannot correctly return transaction status


4️⃣ Proper responsibility split (UVM-clean)
✔ Driver responsibilities

Drive protocol correctly

Wait for completion

Sample transaction result

Return result to sequence / test

✔ Monitor responsibilities

Observe ALL transactions

Create canonical transaction object

Broadcast to scoreboard & coverage

✔ Scoreboard responsibilities

Determine correctness

5️⃣ cocotb makes this more visible (and stricter)

Because cocotb enforces simulation phases:

In cocotb, every simulation timestep is divided into phases.
The two most important ones for DV are:

Write (drive) phase

ReadOnly (sample) phase

Write phase → drive

ReadOnly phase → sample

cocotb rule (very strict)

❌ You must not drive signals in ReadOnly
✅ You should sample signals in ReadOnly

If you don’t explicitly sample in the driver:

You miss the completion window

You get stale data

You saw exactly this bug
   
✅ Correct pattern (memorize this)

await RisingEdge(clk)
await ReadOnly()

if pready:
    sample outputs NOW
    
    
🔑 One-line rule to memorize

Write drives signals, Sample observes stable outputs, Read is just a transaction that contains both.   


📌 Golden Rule

If the monitor sees it, the driver must sample in the SAME ReadOnly phase.


 
🧠 Root cause (the key concept)   AssertionError: Expected PSLVERR=1 on write addr=45, got 0

APB protocol rule:

PSLVERR is only guaranteed valid in the same cycle as PREADY=1 AND PSEL && PENABLE

Your current driver does this (simplified):

Waits for PREADY

Advances a clock

Reads PSLVERR ❌ (too late)

So:

❌ The driver is sampling PSLVERR at the wrong time
✅ The monitor is sampling it at the right time



🧠 Mental model to memorize

Drive before ReadOnly
Sample only in ReadOnly
Never drive in ReadOnly

If you remember only this, you’ll avoid 90% of cocotb timing bugs.

🧩 How this maps to UVM
UVM concept	                                  cocotb equivalent
@(posedge clk)	                                       await RisingEdge()
NBA settle(NBA = Non-Blocking Assignment)  *    	          implicit
Monitor sampling	                                 ReadOnly()
Protocol stability	                            enforced by scheduler

UVM hides this.
cocotb forces you to be explicit.

* All non-blocking assignments scheduled for the current timestep have finished updating signals.

In other words:The design has fully “settled” for this clock edge.


⏱️ Simulation phases (simplified)

At a single clock edge:

1) Active region
   - Blocking assignments (=)
   - Evaluate always blocks

2) NBA region
   - Non-blocking assignments (<=) update

3) NBA settle
   - All <= updates are now visible
   - Combinational logic settles

4) Observation phase
   - Monitors/sample logic read stable signals


📌 NBA settle = after step 2 and 3

🧪 Why this matters for APB (your case)

Signals like:

PREADY

PSLVERR

PRDATA

are often driven via:

sequential logic (<=)

combinational logic depending on registers

So:

If you sample before NBA settle, you may see the previous cycle’s value.


2️⃣ Fundamental rule  

Drivers drive.
Monitors sample.
Drivers should NEVER rely on sampled DUT outputs for checking.

Write vs Read vs Sample (very important distinction)
🔹 WRITE (driver action)

Driver drives signals into the DUT

Example:

psel = 1
penable = 1
paddr = 45
pwdata = 0x3333


This is stimulus, not observation.

🔹 SAMPLE (monitor action)

Monitor observes stable DUT outputs

Happens after NBA settle

Uses:

await RisingEdge(clk)
await ReadOnly()


This is observation, not control.

🔹 READ (APB transaction type)

Logical APB operation

Still split into:

Driver drives bus

DUT responds

Monitor samples response

4️⃣ What “ReadOnly → sample” actually means

When you do:

await ReadOnly()


You are saying:

“Simulator, stop ALL writes.
Let me look at the FINAL, settled values.”

At this point:

All <= assignments are done

All combinational logic has settled

Signals are stable

📌 Monitors belong here
📌 Drivers do NOT

5️⃣ Why your DRIVER is wrong (precise reason)

Your driver does this:

await ReadOnly()
slverr = int(self.dut.pslverr.value)


Then later:

await RisingEdge(self.dut.pclk)
self.dut.psel.value = 0
self.dut.penable.value = 0

❌ The problem

In APB, PSLVERR is only guaranteed valid when:

PSEL && PENABLE && PREADY


Once you advance the clock:

DUT is allowed to deassert PSLVERR

PSLVERR may be 1-cycle wide

Your monitor samples exactly at the valid window
Your driver samples after the protocol window closes

That’s why:

Component	 Result
Monitor      	slverr = 1 ✅
Driver	         slverr = 0 ❌

6️⃣ Correct UVM-style architecture (this is the fix)
❌ What NOT to do (current behavior)

# Driver checking DUT outputs
assert slverr == 1


Drivers should never assert protocol correctness.

✅ Correct architecture (UVM-accurate)
Driver

Drives APB

Returns intent, not DUT truth

await drv.write(addr, data, expect_slverr=True)

Monitor

Observes DUT

Creates transactions

ApbTxn(kind="WRITE", addr=45, slverr=1)

Scoreboard

Compares expected vs observed

if txn.addr >= 32:
    assert txn.slverr == 1


📌 All checking belongs in the scoreboard

7️⃣ One-sentence rule to memorize (this will save your career)

Drivers never trust DUT outputs; monitors observe truth, scoreboards judge correctness.

8️⃣ Why Verilator makes this visible (and other sims hide it)

Commercial simulators:

Often keep signals stable longer

Mask timing mistakes

Verilator:

Cycle-accurate

Protocol-strict

Exposes bad sampling immediately

This is why Verilator + cocotb is elite training.
